WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.035
Cookies are security-sensitive state carriers.

2
00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:04.355
In ASP.

3
00:00:04.355 --> 00:00:12.932
NET Core they may support authentication, antiforgery protection, correlation flows, preferences, or application session behavior.

4
00:00:12.932 --> 00:00:21.839
A cookie can influence who the application thinks the user is, which workflow is in progress, or whether a browser request is accepted.

5
00:00:21.839 --> 00:00:26.920
Cookie configuration therefore deserves review, not copy-and-paste treatment.

6
00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:32.726
Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite, expiration, sliding expiration, and cookie scope all matter.

7
00:00:32.726 --> 00:00:36.817
Secure helps ensure cookies are sent over protected transport.

8
00:00:36.817 --> 00:00:39.786
HttpOnly reduces exposure to browser scripts.

9
00:00:39.786 --> 00:00:42.953
SameSite influences cross-site sending behavior.

10
00:00:42.953 --> 00:00:46.780
Expiration and sliding expiration affect session lifetime.

11
00:00:46.780 --> 00:00:50.343
Scope controls which host or path receives the cookie.

12
00:00:50.343 --> 00:00:55.423
Each setting should match the application's deployment and user expectations.

13
00:00:55.423 --> 00:01:03.341
CSRF is best understood in safe terms: a browser can send existing credentials with a cross-site state-changing request.

14
00:01:03.341 --> 00:01:14.953
Antiforgery protections help the application distinguish an intentional user action in the application from a cross-site request that arrives with existing browser credentials.

15
00:01:14.953 --> 00:01:22.409
State-changing browser workflows should be reviewed for antiforgery behavior, method choice, and cookie settings.

16
00:01:22.409 --> 00:01:23.729
CORS is different.

17
00:01:23.729 --> 00:01:27.886
It is a browser-enforced cross-origin access control mechanism.

18
00:01:27.886 --> 00:01:34.088
It controls which origins can read certain responses from browser-based cross-origin requests.

19
00:01:34.088 --> 00:01:39.498
It is not authentication, and it does not prove user identity or service identity.

20
00:01:39.498 --> 00:01:47.350
A permissive CORS policy can increase exposure when combined with sensitive responses or credentialed browser requests.

21
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:51.111
Avoid broad origins and credentials without a clear need.

22
00:01:51.111 --> 00:01:58.434
A narrowly scoped CORS policy is easier to reason about than a policy that trusts many origins for convenience.

23
00:01:58.434 --> 00:02:09.915
Teams should document which front-end origins are approved, whether credentials are required, which methods and headers are allowed, and how this changes across environments.

24
00:02:09.915 --> 00:02:12.158
Session state should be minimized.

25
00:02:12.158 --> 00:02:19.680
Storing large or sensitive values in session can create privacy, scaling, troubleshooting, and lifecycle problems.

26
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:25.816
Prefer storing only what is necessary and keeping authoritative decisions on the server side.

27
00:02:25.816 --> 00:02:33.866
If session data influences access, workflow state, or sensitive behavior, it should be treated as security-relevant state.
